hypotheses.statement:
On any two-sided word and for any integers $t,q$, $$\label{eq:inner-ambiguity}
 \#\{(p,p')\in\mathcal P_0^2:p\neq p',\
                         A_0(t+p-p',q)\}\leq C_0.$$ The same bound holds on finite words whenever all the indicated inspection windows are internal.

On a two-sided word, uniformly in $t$, $$\label{eq:end-ambiguity}
 \#\bigl\{(p,p')\in\mathcal P_1^2:p\ne p',\
       \exists\gamma\in\Delta_0\ A_1(t+p-p'+\gamma)\bigr\}
 \le C_1.$$ The same estimate holds on a finite word whenever all the indicated inspection windows are present.

outputs.exact_statement:
Suppose $r$ is usable at $t$, and $h$ is a multiple of $\delta_r$ with $|h|\leq R_r$. Write $B_j^{(h)}=B(z_j+h)$. Then $$\label{eq:transport-intervals}
        T_{B_{j-1},B_j}=T_{B_{j-1}^{(h)},B_j^{(h)}}
                         \qquad(1\leq j\leq l).$$ For a fixed endpoint $b\geq z_l+H$, the last transfer also satisfies $$\label{eq:transport-tail}
                         T_{B_l,b}=T_{B_l^{(h)},b}.$$ These statements apply to finite words whenever the prescribed windows and product intervals are available. They hold for arbitrary finite monoids, without a commutativity assumption.

